From 2d1e6dc1269eb79abb072d08475e909b9ce507c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 11:09:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] agentHost: reclaim orphaned session data directories

Every external session discovered from a provider catalogue gets a data
directory holding a schema-only session.db, because seeding its read state
opens the database. When the startup prune later unregistered a row older
than 30 days it never deleted that directory, and nothing else ever could:
once the registry row is gone the directory cannot be matched to a session
again. On one machine 96% of the session data directories were such
leftovers.

The prune now deletes the session data of every row it removes. A startup
sweep queued behind the prune hands the registered session URIs, plus live
sessions and in-process suppressed chat backings, to the previously unused
cleanupOrphanedData, which now takes URIs and derives directory names
itself. A directory is kept when it belongs to a known session, to one of
its subagent sessions, or to one of its chat channels (keyed
<chatId>-<base64url(session)>), and also when its database carries the chat
backing marker, since backing SDK sessions are never registered. The marker
key moves to sessionState so both sides share it.

Nothing is deleted when the registry cannot be enumerated (the failure
propagates before the sweep is asked to run) or when it lists no sessions
at all, so a lost or fresh registry never wipes a populated data directory.
